// Practice quiz: Functional interfaces and anonymous classes
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, and code is described in words where GIFT syntax would clash.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/inheritance-and-design/functional-interfaces-and-anonymous-classes

::o1 one abstract method::Which interface is a functional interface? {
~One with two abstract methods#Not quite. A functional interface has exactly one abstract method.
=One that declares exactly one abstract method and any number of default methods#Correct. Default and static methods do not count toward the single abstract method.
~One with no methods at all#Not quite. It needs one abstract method to implement.
~Any interface marked public#Not quite. Visibility is unrelated.
}

::o1 annotation::What does @FunctionalInterface add? {
=A compile-time check that the interface has exactly one abstract method#Correct. The interface would be functional without it; the annotation guards against later mistakes.
~It makes an interface functional that otherwise would not be#Not quite. The method count decides, not the annotation.
~It allows a second abstract method#Not quite. It forbids that.
~It turns the interface into a class#Not quite. It stays an interface.
}

::o2 anonymous class::What does an anonymous class expression such as new Greeter() followed by a class body create? {
~An instance of the Greeter interface itself#Not quite. Interfaces cannot be instantiated; the unnamed class is.
~A named class called Greeter#Not quite. The class has no name.
=An instance of a new unnamed class that implements Greeter#Correct. The body supplies the method implementations on the spot.
~A lambda#Not quite. It is a full class body, though a lambda can often replace it.
}

::o2 captured variables::An anonymous class uses a local variable from the enclosing method. What rule applies? {
~The variable must be static#Not quite. Local variables cannot be static.
~The variable can be reassigned freely#Not quite. Reassigning it makes the capture a compile error.
~Anonymous classes cannot use local variables#Not quite. They can, if the variables are effectively final.
=The variable must be final or effectively final#Correct. The class captures a copy, so the local variable must not change.
}

::o3 Predicate::Which standard interface represents a test that returns true or false for one value? {
=Predicate, with its test method#Correct. Predicate of T takes a T and returns a boolean.
~Supplier#Not quite. Supplier takes nothing and returns a value.
~Function#Not quite. Function returns a result of any type, not specifically boolean.
~Runnable#Not quite. Runnable takes nothing and returns nothing.
}

::o3 Supplier::A method needs a way to produce a fresh default value on demand, with no input. Which standard interface fits? {
~Predicate#Not quite. Predicate takes a value and returns a boolean.
~Function#Not quite. Function needs an input value.
=Supplier, with its get method#Correct. Supplier of T takes no arguments and returns a T.
~Consumer#Not quite. Consumer takes a value and returns nothing.
}
